{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS3zBrcm8DeRy1Z5Mwnz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QMga93597Fs9AU5cVYrcXZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-Sqttzp; python3 - <<'E'\np='src/app.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"import time\\n\",\"import time\\nfrom decimal import Decimal\\n\",1)\ns=s.replace('\"score\": similarity(story, other)})','\"score\": Decimal(str(similarity(story, other)))})')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='tests/test_publish.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('\\n\\nif __name__','''\n    def test_second_publish_stores_similar_scores(self):\n        table = FakeTable()\n        app.publish(table, {\"slug\": \"first-story\", \"title\": \"A\", \"tags\": [\"cron\", \"linux\"], \"body\": \"cron runs twice\"})\n        stored = app.publish(table, {\"slug\": \"second-story\", \"title\": \"B\", \"tags\": [\"cron\"], \"body\": \"cron ran once\"})\n        self.assertEqual(stored[\"similar\"][0][\"slug\"], \"first-story\")\n        self.assertEqual(table.scan()[\"Count\"], 2)\n\n\nif __name__''',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Second publish returns 500\n\n**Cause:** `related()` in `src/app.py` put `score` values into the item as Python floats (`similarity()` returns `round(..., 4)`). boto3's DynamoDB serializer rejects floats (`TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`). The first publish worked only because the table was empty, so `similar` was `[]` and no float was written. From the second publish on, `similar` holds scored entries, so `put_item` fails and the handler returns 500.\n\n**Fix:** `related()` now stores each score as `Decimal(str(score))`. `str()` avoids binary-float noise. I added a regression test (`test_second_publish_stores_similar_scores`) that publishes twice. The fake table rejects floats the way boto3 does, so the test fails on the old code.\n\n**Not fixed:** `publish()` calls `scan` once and doesn't follow `LastEvaluatedKey`. Once the table passes 1 MB, `similar` will be computed from only part of the library. That is a separate problem.\nE\npython3 -m unittest discover tests 2>&1 | tail -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":807,"cache_read_input_tokens":23097,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":807},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9895c35d-0147-4937-b0e7-7c833ed34ea2","uuid":"04313e31-55e7-4497-9136-2b5180c7e20d","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:43:59.136Z","request_id":"req_011CgS3zBbjVyJDcY5334tCE","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QMga93597Fs9AU5cVYrcXZ":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-Sqttzp; python3 - <<'E'\np='src/app.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"import time\\n\",\"import time\\nfrom decimal import Decimal\\n\",1)\ns=s.replace('\"score\": similarity(story, other)})','\"score\": Decimal(str(similarity(story, other)))})')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='tests/test_publish.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('\\n\\nif __name__','''\n    def test_second_publish_stores_similar_scores(self):\n        table = FakeTable()\n        app.publish(table, {\"slug\": \"first-story\", \"title\": \"A\", \"tags\": [\"cron\", \"linux\"], \"body\": \"cron runs twice\"})\n        stored = app.publish(table, {\"slug\": \"second-story\", \"title\": \"B\", \"tags\": [\"cron\"], \"body\": \"cron ran once\"})\n        self.assertEqual(stored[\"similar\"][0][\"slug\"], \"first-story\")\n        self.assertEqual(table.scan()[\"Count\"], 2)\n\n\nif __name__''',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Second publish returns 500\n\n**Cause:** `related()` in `src/app.py` put `score` values into the item as Python floats (`similarity()` returns `round(..., 4)`). boto3's DynamoDB serializer rejects floats (`TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`). The first publish worked only because the table was empty, so `similar` was `[]` and no float was written. From the second publish on, `similar` holds scored entries, so `put_item` fails and the handler returns 500.\n\n**Fix:** `related()` now stores each score as `Decimal(str(score))`. `str()` avoids binary-float noise. I added a regression test (`test_second_publish_stores_similar_scores`) that publishes twice. The fake table rejects floats the way boto3 does, so the test fails on the old code.\n\n**Not fixed:** `publish()` calls `scan` once and doesn't follow `LastEvaluatedKey`. Once the table passes 1 MB, `similar` will be computed from only part of the library. That is a separate problem.\nE\npython3 -m unittest discover tests 2>&1 | tail -3"}}}
